[THOUGHT]  
The clinical picture centers on a painless goiter accompanied by classic sympathetic overactivity (sweating, insomnia, irritability, fatigue) — hallmark features of hyperthyroidism. The provisional diagnosis favors **Graves’ disease**, the most common cause of *diffuse*, *autoimmune*, *hyperthyroid* goiter. Key supporting features include:  
- Painless (non-inflammatory) thyroid enlargement;  
- Absence of weight loss or overt cardiac symptoms suggests *mild or early* disease — consistent with subclinical or incipient overt hyperthyroidism;  
- Exclusion of alternatives:  
  • Toxic nodular goiter is less likely without evidence of autonomy (e.g., “hot” nodule on scan) or older age predilection;  
  • Silent (painless) thyroiditis typically follows a transient, self-limited course with antecedent viral illness or postpartum timing — neither mentioned;  
  • TSH-secreting adenoma is ruled out by expected *elevated* TSH in that setting — yet TSH is presumed suppressed (per context: “suppressed TSH + elevated free T4/T3 would confirm”), making it highly unlikely.  
The emphasis on *diffuse* goiter, autoimmunity, and ophthalmic surveillance (implied by “eye signs”) further strengthens Graves’ as the leading etiology. While definitive confirmation requires positive TSH receptor antibodies (TRAb) and characteristic ultrasound findings (e.g., diffuse hypoechogenicity, increased vascularity), the constellation — especially in a patient with goiter + adrenergic symptoms + biochemical hyperthyroidism (pending Free T4/T3) — makes Graves’ disease the single most coherent, statistically predominant, and mechanistically consistent diagnosis.

[DIAGNOSIS]  
Graves’ disease

